1
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:06,280
Hey, do me a favor and take Table Seven.

2
00:00:07,660 --> 00:00:09,700
You mean the one with my 118-pound

3
00:00:09,700 --> 00:00:11,450
rock-hard stud of a fiance

4
00:00:11,450 --> 00:00:13,970
who's prone to canker sores and pinkeye?

5
00:00:15,130 --> 00:00:17,630
Nah, I prefer to look at it as the one with my ex-boyfriend

6
00:00:17,630 --> 00:00:20,180
and his gorgeous, successful and sophisticated girlfriend,

7
00:00:20,180 --> 00:00:22,270
who makes me feel like a toothless Okie.

8
00:00:24,750 --> 00:00:27,090
Do you want me to spill hot soup on her?

9
00:00:28,190 --> 00:00:30,350
Oh, please-- you're not that kind of person.

10
00:00:30,350 --> 00:00:31,430
I know.

11
00:00:31,430 --> 00:00:33,420
But if she orders something low-fat

12
00:00:33,420 --> 00:00:36,160
I'll totally give her the full-fat version.

13
00:00:38,210 --> 00:00:40,090
Uh, that's my water.

14
00:00:40,090 --> 00:00:40,840
What?

15
00:00:40,840 --> 00:00:42,150
My water. You're drinking it.

16
00:00:42,150 --> 00:00:43,950
D-Dear Lord!

17
00:00:45,480 --> 00:00:46,880
Have you been drinking it?

18
00:00:46,880 --> 00:00:48,790
Yes. It's my water.

19
00:00:48,790 --> 00:00:50,220
Well, that's it then.

20
00:00:50,220 --> 00:00:51,420
I'm dead.

21
00:00:52,880 --> 00:00:53,980
Here we go...

22
00:00:53,980 --> 00:00:56,890
I'm sorry, do I really need to connect the dots for you?

23
00:00:56,890 --> 00:00:58,660
The backwash into this glass

24
00:00:58,660 --> 00:01:01,980
is every pathogen that calls your mouth home, sweet home.

25
00:01:01,980 --> 00:01:04,550
Not to mention the visitors who arrive on the dancing tongue

26
00:01:04,550 --> 00:01:06,640
of your subtropical girlfriend.

27
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:09,400
Hey! That's my sister

28
00:01:09,400 --> 00:01:11,250
and my country you're talking about.

29
00:01:11,250 --> 00:01:12,530
Leonard may have defiled one,

30
00:01:12,530 --> 00:01:15,160
but I won't have you talking smack about the other.

31
00:01:16,050 --> 00:01:17,190
You guys ready to order?

32
00:01:17,190 --> 00:01:19,990
Yes, I'd like a seven-day course of penicillin,

33
00:01:19,990 --> 00:01:22,340
some, uh, syrup of ipecac-- to induce vomiting.

34
00:01:22,340 --> 00:01:23,720
and a mint.

35
00:01:25,250 --> 00:01:26,280
I don't understand.

36
00:01:26,280 --> 00:01:27,760
He drank from Leonard's glass.

37
00:01:27,760 --> 00:01:29,110
"He drank from Leonard's glass"--

38
00:01:29,110 --> 00:01:31,300
the words they'll be carving into my tombstone.

39
00:01:33,020 --> 00:01:34,670
That's actually my napkin.

40
00:01:34,670 --> 00:01:36,460
Oh, this is a nightmare!

41
00:01:36,460 --> 00:01:38,530
- Where are you going? - To the bar,

42
00:01:38,530 --> 00:01:41,140
to sterilize my mouth with alcohol.

43
00:01:41,140 --> 00:01:43,070
Gang way! Dead man walking!

44
00:02:09,960 --> 00:02:11,210
All right. Here you go.

45
00:02:11,400 --> 00:02:13,020
Two lemonades,

46
00:02:13,020 --> 00:02:14,710
one iced tea

47
00:02:14,710 --> 00:02:16,980
and a root beer for Priya.

48
00:02:16,980 --> 00:02:19,950
- Is it diet? - That's what you ordered.

49
00:02:22,920 --> 00:02:23,850
Thank you.

50
00:02:23,850 --> 00:02:24,870
Hey, have you and Howard

51
00:02:24,870 --> 00:02:26,730
started planning your wedding yet?

52
00:02:26,730 --> 00:02:28,640
Yeah. We're thinking of having it on a cliff

53
00:02:28,640 --> 00:02:30,620
overlooking the ocean.

54
00:02:30,620 --> 00:02:32,510
Nothing soothes those pre-wedding jitters

55
00:02:32,510 --> 00:02:34,570
like the thought of falling and drowning.

56
00:02:36,380 --> 00:02:37,310
What did your mother say

57
00:02:37,310 --> 00:02:39,150
when you told her you were getting married?

58
00:02:39,150 --> 00:02:40,270
He hasn't told her yet.

59
00:02:40,270 --> 00:02:41,480
He's waiting for the right time.

60
00:02:41,480 --> 00:02:44,550
I was thinking of weaving it into her eulogy.

61
00:02:45,880 --> 00:02:48,160
Howard, you've got to tell your mother.

62
00:02:48,160 --> 00:02:49,500
Hey, have you told your parents you're dating

63
00:02:49,500 --> 00:02:51,400
this short glass of skim milk here?

64
00:02:53,450 --> 00:02:55,650
Uh... that's different.

65
00:02:55,650 --> 00:02:57,500
First of all, we're not engaged;

66
00:02:57,500 --> 00:02:59,180
and second, Indian parents

67
00:02:59,180 --> 00:03:01,090
are very protective of their children.

68
00:03:01,090 --> 00:03:03,020
Right, right, whereas Jewish mothers

69
00:03:03,020 --> 00:03:05,820
take a casual, la-di-da approach to their sons.

70
00:03:09,390 --> 00:03:12,630
I gargled with tequila and may have swallowed a teeny bit.

71
00:03:12,630 --> 00:03:13,900
- You all right? - Fine.

72
00:03:13,900 --> 00:03:15,420
Thank you for asking.

73
00:03:15,420 --> 00:03:17,400
I love you so much.

74
00:03:21,150 --> 00:03:22,510
Fire demon.

75
00:03:23,080 --> 00:03:24,620
Ooh, fire demon.

76
00:03:24,620 --> 00:03:27,840
Sheldon's turning up the heat...

77
00:03:29,520 --> 00:03:30,770
Troll master.

78
00:03:30,770 --> 00:03:31,820
Check it! Howard pulls one out

79
00:03:31,820 --> 00:03:34,250
from under the bridge. Nice!

80
00:03:36,680 --> 00:03:37,750
Water nymph.

81
00:03:37,750 --> 00:03:38,660
Oh, yeah, she's got puddles

82
00:03:38,660 --> 00:03:40,600
in all the right places.

83
00:03:41,910 --> 00:03:42,840
Could you please play the game

84
00:03:42,840 --> 00:03:45,370
without commenting on every card?

85
00:03:45,370 --> 00:03:47,210
Sorry.

86
00:03:47,210 --> 00:03:48,800
Walking tree.

87
00:03:53,690 --> 00:03:54,430
Last one.

88
00:03:54,430 --> 00:03:56,770
I'm taking a stroll and I'm sporting wood.

89
00:04:02,380 --> 00:04:04,850
Okay, the Eagle has landed.

90
00:04:04,850 --> 00:04:05,730
What's going on?

91
00:04:05,730 --> 00:04:07,080
Bernadette and my mother are having

92
00:04:07,080 --> 00:04:09,520
a get-to-know-you lunch at the Old Town Deli.

93
00:04:09,520 --> 00:04:10,820
Oh, that sounds lovely.

94
00:04:10,820 --> 00:04:11,680
Hope so.

95
00:04:11,680 --> 00:04:12,980
Of course, if history is any indication,

96
00:04:12,980 --> 00:04:14,110
my mother will swallow her whole

97
00:04:14,110 --> 00:04:16,110
and spit out her bones like an owl.

98
00:04:18,710 --> 00:04:20,160
Have you met Bernadette's parents?

99
00:04:20,160 --> 00:04:21,850
You mean Adolf and Eva?

100
00:04:24,240 --> 00:04:24,850
Not yet.

101
00:04:24,850 --> 00:04:26,810
One goose step at a time.

102
00:04:28,280 --> 00:04:29,820
Sheldon, that's my water.

103
00:04:29,820 --> 00:04:31,800
Oh, dear Lord!

104
00:04:35,270 --> 00:04:36,840
That's not your water.

105
00:04:37,630 --> 00:04:38,980
I know.

106
00:04:41,070 --> 00:04:43,290
Where's the mouthwash?!

107
00:04:44,150 --> 00:04:45,930
"Where" indeed.

108
00:04:48,980 --> 00:04:51,060
Ma, I'm home!

109
00:04:52,080 --> 00:04:53,200
Where are you?

110
00:04:53,200 --> 00:04:55,530
I'm in the toilet!

111
00:04:56,920 --> 00:04:58,610
So, how'd it go?!

112
00:04:58,610 --> 00:04:59,990
Too soon to say!

113
00:04:59,990 --> 00:05:01,790
I'm not done yet!

114
00:05:04,190 --> 00:05:07,040
No, I mean lunch, today, with Bernadette!

115
00:05:07,040 --> 00:05:10,320
I had a pastrami sandwich.

116
00:05:10,320 --> 00:05:12,680
She had eggplant lasagna.

117
00:05:12,680 --> 00:05:16,020
Like that's what a person orders in a Jewish deli.

118
00:05:17,410 --> 00:05:19,670
Besides food, did you get along?

119
00:05:19,670 --> 00:05:20,510
Did you talk?

120
00:05:20,510 --> 00:05:24,030
Oh, sure! Did you know she's going to school

121
00:05:24,030 --> 00:05:27,560
to become a microbiologist?!

122
00:05:27,560 --> 00:05:30,290
No, she never mentioned it.

123
00:05:31,300 --> 00:05:34,490
I bet she did and you didn't listen!

124
00:05:36,360 --> 00:05:38,400
Yeah, that's probably it. So, what do you think?

125
00:05:38,400 --> 00:05:40,600
Do you like her? She's great, huh?

126
00:05:40,600 --> 00:05:42,320
She's a lovely girl!

127
00:05:42,320 --> 00:05:43,970
Cute as a button!

128
00:05:43,970 --> 00:05:48,190
That's good to hear, 'cause I've got some news.

129
00:05:48,190 --> 00:05:49,830
I hope it's good news,

130
00:05:49,830 --> 00:05:53,080
because I've got nothing but disappointment in here!

131
00:06:01,610 --> 00:06:05,450
Bernadette and I are getting married.

132
00:06:09,410 --> 00:06:11,090
Ma?

133
00:06:11,940 --> 00:06:14,750
You too busy bearing down?

134
00:06:16,450 --> 00:06:18,050
Ma?

135
00:06:20,680 --> 00:06:22,600
Oh, my God, Ma? Ma? Ma?

136
00:06:22,600 --> 00:06:24,400
Stand back, I'm gonna break the door down!

137
00:06:32,620 --> 00:06:35,270
Son of a bitch! Ma, help!

138
00:06:39,910 --> 00:06:41,180
Hello.

139
00:06:41,180 --> 00:06:42,330
Haven't seen you in a while.

140
00:06:42,330 --> 00:06:43,440
How's it going?

141
00:06:43,440 --> 00:06:46,610
Oh, other than waiting out the exponential growth period

142
00:06:46,610 --> 00:06:48,420
of the virulent organisms

143
00:06:48,420 --> 00:06:50,180
trooping through my microvilli

144
00:06:50,180 --> 00:06:54,180
into my circulatory system -- hunky-dory.

145
00:06:59,560 --> 00:07:01,730
Did I say something amusing?

146
00:07:01,730 --> 00:07:04,230
I don't know, maybe, I have no idea what you said.

147
00:07:05,240 --> 00:07:08,270
So your mirth is merely a discharge of nervous energy

148
00:07:08,270 --> 00:07:11,140
with no semantic content at all?

149
00:07:11,140 --> 00:07:13,690
My mirth. Classic.

150
00:07:14,720 --> 00:07:16,170
Is there a station coming up

151
00:07:16,170 --> 00:07:19,000
where I can board your giggling train of thought?

152
00:07:20,180 --> 00:07:21,670
It's not a big deal, Sheldon.

153
00:07:21,670 --> 00:07:24,400
It's just, ever since Leonard's been dating Raj's sister,

154
00:07:24,400 --> 00:07:26,300
 I've had to keep my distance.

155
00:07:26,300 --> 00:07:28,660
I don't get to hear all your jibber-jabber.

156
00:07:28,910 --> 00:07:30,130
Jibber-jabber?!

157
00:07:30,130 --> 00:07:32,590
I don't jibber-jabber.

158
00:07:32,590 --> 00:07:34,280
What are you doing at work these days?

159
00:07:34,280 --> 00:07:36,890
Oh, I'm working on time-dependent backgrounds

160
00:07:36,890 --> 00:07:38,040
in string theory,

161
00:07:38,040 --> 00:07:40,030
specifically quantum field theory

162
00:07:40,030 --> 00:07:43,140
in "d-dimensional" de Sitter space.

163
00:07:43,770 --> 00:07:47,160
Okay, come on, even you have to admit that's jibber-jabber.

164
00:07:48,520 --> 00:07:49,920
Interesting, do you know

165
00:07:49,920 --> 00:07:52,840
where the phrase "jibber-jabber" comes from?

166
00:07:52,840 --> 00:07:54,720
Oh, my God, you're about to jibber-jabber

167
00:07:54,720 --> 00:07:56,600
about jibber-jabber.

168
00:08:00,410 --> 00:08:01,730
Howard and his mother are at the hospital.

169
00:08:01,730 --> 00:08:03,430
- Come on, we gotta go.  - Oh, my God! What happened?

170
00:08:03,430 --> 00:08:05,070
I don't know, I just got a text. Come on, hurry.

171
00:08:05,070 --> 00:08:06,440
Okay.

172
00:08:10,150 --> 00:08:12,100
Sheldon, let's go!

173
00:08:12,100 --> 00:08:14,030
To a hospital?!

174
00:08:14,030 --> 00:08:15,960
Full of sick people?!

175
00:08:16,750 --> 00:08:18,460
Oh, I don't think so.

176
00:08:19,180 --> 00:08:20,560
Okay, well, your friend and his mother are there,

177
00:08:20,560 --> 00:08:22,400
we're going.

178
00:08:22,400 --> 00:08:24,000
I can't.

179
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:26,110
Oh, don't tell me you're afraid of germs.

180
00:08:26,110 --> 00:08:28,120
Not all germs.

181
00:08:28,950 --> 00:08:30,960
Just the ones that will kill me.

182
00:08:31,520 --> 00:08:34,130
It's the same way I'm not afraid of all steak knives --

183
00:08:34,130 --> 00:08:36,650
just the ones that might be plunged in my thorax.

184
00:08:37,770 --> 00:08:39,450
Fine, I'll tell Howard you didn't come

185
00:08:39,450 --> 00:08:40,350
because you're more concerned

186
00:08:40,350 --> 00:08:42,210
about your own well-being than his.

187
00:08:42,210 --> 00:08:44,870
I would think he would know that.

188
00:08:46,480 --> 00:08:48,530
Okay, you know what? You are unbelievable.

189
00:08:48,530 --> 00:08:50,480
You buy all these superhero T-shirts,

190
00:08:50,480 --> 00:08:51,240
but when it's time for you

191
00:08:51,240 --> 00:08:52,670
to step up and do the right thing,

192
00:08:52,670 --> 00:08:55,050
you just hide in the laundry room.

193
00:08:56,130 --> 00:08:58,040
Fine, I'll go.

194
00:08:58,550 --> 00:08:59,780
Just for the record,

195
00:08:59,780 --> 00:09:02,250
my Aunt Ruth died in a hospital.

196
00:09:02,620 --> 00:09:04,320
She went in to visit my Uncle Roger,

197
00:09:04,320 --> 00:09:07,770
caught something and bit the dust a week later.

198
00:09:08,170 --> 00:09:09,280
The two of them now share a coffee can

199
00:09:09,280 --> 00:09:11,050
on my mother's mantel.

200
00:09:16,180 --> 00:09:17,960
So I knocked down the bathroom door,

201
00:09:18,190 --> 00:09:19,560
picked up my poor unconscious mother,

202
00:09:19,560 --> 00:09:20,380
carried her to the car,

203
00:09:20,380 --> 00:09:23,190
and drove like a madman to the emergency room.

204
00:09:23,780 --> 00:09:25,340
You're a real hero, Howard.

205
00:09:25,340 --> 00:09:27,840
No, I did what any son would do.

206
00:09:27,840 --> 00:09:31,560
Hang on a second -- you picked up your mother?

207
00:09:33,020 --> 00:09:36,060
Her own legs are barely able to do that.

208
00:09:37,350 --> 00:09:39,460
I was filled with adrenaline.

209
00:09:39,460 --> 00:09:42,080
It happens to be how women lift cars off babies.

210
00:09:42,080 --> 00:09:44,650
Yeah, I'm saying, it'd be easier to lift a car.

211
00:09:46,330 --> 00:09:48,520
What can I tell you? After I found the courage

212
00:09:48,520 --> 00:09:51,580
to put her pants back on, I was unstoppable.

213
00:09:53,440 --> 00:09:54,940
So, how is she?

214
00:09:54,940 --> 00:09:56,950
They're running tests-- I don't know.

215
00:09:56,950 --> 00:10:00,720
It may have been a heart attack or heart-attack-like event.

216
00:10:00,720 --> 00:10:01,690
What's the difference?

217
00:10:01,690 --> 00:10:03,460
A heart-attack-like event

218
00:10:03,460 --> 00:10:06,430
is an event that's like a heart attack.

219
00:10:07,400 --> 00:10:09,170
Thanks for clearing that up.

220
00:10:09,170 --> 00:10:12,210
Regardless, coronary problems are eminently treatable.

221
00:10:12,210 --> 00:10:14,360
What's more likely going to kill Howard's mother

222
00:10:14,360 --> 00:10:16,820
are the antibiotic-resistant super-bugs

223
00:10:16,820 --> 00:10:19,850
festering in every nook and cranny of this hospital.

224
00:10:20,690 --> 00:10:22,470
Okay, you're not helping.

225
00:10:22,470 --> 00:10:24,110
Disagree.

226
00:10:25,260 --> 00:10:27,380
Go sit over there.

227
00:10:28,350 --> 00:10:30,610
I'll sit over there-- it looks cleaner.

228
00:10:33,250 --> 00:10:35,810
Is there a history of heart disease in your family?

229
00:10:35,810 --> 00:10:39,080
My family is the history of heart disease.

230
00:10:39,930 --> 00:10:41,020
There's a cave painting in France

231
00:10:41,020 --> 00:10:44,280
of one of my ancestors doing this:

232
00:10:47,310 --> 00:10:49,170
So it's probably genetic.

233
00:10:49,170 --> 00:10:50,530
Well, maybe.

234
00:10:50,530 --> 00:10:54,510
Mom also had just gotten some news that might have upset her.

235
00:10:54,510 --> 00:10:55,790
What?

236
00:10:55,790 --> 00:10:57,350
It's not important.

237
00:10:57,350 --> 00:10:58,820
Come on, Howard, I'm going to be your wife.

238
00:10:58,820 --> 00:11:00,350
You can share anything with me.

239
00:11:00,350 --> 00:11:03,570
You'd think that. But no.

240
00:11:07,670 --> 00:11:09,340
You told her we were going to get married

241
00:11:09,340 --> 00:11:11,140
and she had a heart attack?

242
00:11:11,140 --> 00:11:13,910
You can't take that personally.

243
00:11:15,170 --> 00:11:17,860
How else is she supposed to take it?

244
00:11:17,860 --> 00:11:20,410
What you've got to keep in mind is that

245
00:11:20,410 --> 00:11:22,270
ever since my dad left,

246
00:11:22,270 --> 00:11:24,830
I've been the whole world to my mother.

247
00:11:24,830 --> 00:11:26,680
I mean, she'd be threatened by any woman

248
00:11:26,680 --> 00:11:29,430
who can give me what she can't.

249
00:11:29,840 --> 00:11:32,400
You mean sexual intercourse?

250
00:11:35,180 --> 00:11:38,170
Well, when you say it like that you make it sound creepy.

251
00:11:38,910 --> 00:11:40,170
What happened?

252
00:11:40,170 --> 00:11:41,730
Howard's mother had a heart attack

253
00:11:41,730 --> 00:11:44,950
because I have sex with him and she can't.

254
00:11:48,530 --> 00:11:50,210
Bernie, wait!

255
00:11:51,900 --> 00:11:55,010
I'll give you $1,000 if you'll drive me home.

256
00:11:57,700 --> 00:11:59,700
Nice of you to come for Howard.

257
00:11:59,700 --> 00:12:02,020
Well, he's my friend. It's what you do.

258
00:12:05,200 --> 00:12:07,180
I'm sorry, did I miss something?

259
00:12:07,180 --> 00:12:08,450
It's just so weird.

260
00:12:08,450 --> 00:12:10,810
Howard Wolowitz is my friend.

261
00:12:10,810 --> 00:12:12,310
You know, once he tried to stick his tongue down my throat

262
00:12:12,310 --> 00:12:14,400
and I broke his nose.

263
00:12:15,370 --> 00:12:18,770
That's a little easier to believe than he's your friend.

264
00:12:18,770 --> 00:12:20,430
Yeah, tell me about it.

265
00:12:20,430 --> 00:12:23,080
You know, my brother had a group of friends like this in India.

266
00:12:23,080 --> 00:12:24,920
They dressed up in leotards and goggles

267
00:12:24,920 --> 00:12:27,850
and called themselves the New Delhi Power Rangers.

268
00:12:29,390 --> 00:12:30,780
You mean when he was little.

269
00:12:30,780 --> 00:12:33,550
Not as little as you'd want him to be.

270
00:12:35,910 --> 00:12:37,310
You know, this stuff is horrible.

271
00:12:37,310 --> 00:12:38,650
